<tool id="abritamr" name="abriTAMR" version="1.3.0+galaxy0" profile="21.05">
  <description>Antimicrobial resistance gene detection</description>
  <macros />
  <xrefs>
    <xref type="bio.tools">abritamr</xref>
  </xrefs>
  <requirements>
    <requirement type="package" version="1.3.0">abritamr</requirement>
  </requirements>
  <command detect_errors="exit_code">
        abritamr run
        --input '$input'
        --output '$output'
        --sop '$sop'
        --threads \${GALAXY_SLOTS:-1}
    </command>
  <inputs>
    <param name="input" type="data" format="fasta" label="Input sequence(s)" />
    <param name="sop" type="select" label="Standard operating procedure">
      <option value="general">General</option>
      <option value="plus">Plus</option>
    </param>
  </inputs>
  <outputs>
    <data name="output" format="tabular" label="${tool.name} on ${on_string}: abriTAMR output" />
  </outputs>
  <tests>
    <test>
      <param name="input" value="test.fasta" />
      <param name="sop" value="general" />
      <output name="output" file="general_test.tabular" />
    </test>
  </tests>
  <help>
*Taming the AMR beast*

abriTAMR is an AMR gene detection pipeline that runs AMRFinderPlus on a single (or list ) of given isolates and collates the results into a table, separating genes identified into functionally relevant groups. abriTAMR is accredited by NATA for use in reporting the presence of reportable AMR genes in Victoria Australia.
    + Acquired resistance mechanims in the form of point mutations (restricted to subset of species)
    + Streamlined output.
    + Presence of virulence factors
    </help>
  <citations>
    <citation type="doi">10.1093/genevo/gov010</citation>
    <citation type="bibtex">@misc{galaxy_toolsmith,
  title = {Galaxy Toolsmith},
  howpublished = {\url{https://github.com/BlankenbergLab/galaxy-toolsmith}},
  url = {https://github.com/BlankenbergLab/galaxy-toolsmith}
}</citation>
  </citations>
</tool>